rc("The flexible worker's bargain", "m", [
"The workers who deliver food, drive taxis or clean homes through digital platforms are often described as entrepreneurs. They choose their hours, accept or decline jobs and can work for several platforms at once. Platforms present this freedom as the essence of the arrangement, and many workers value it: surveys regularly find that flexibility is the feature they would least like to give up.",
"Yet the freedom is shaped by an algorithm the worker cannot see. A courier who declines too many orders may find fewer offered; a driver who logs off during busy hours may lose the bonus that made the evening worthwhile. Pay per job can change without notice, and the rating systems that decide who gets work are run by the platform alone. The worker is free to choose, but the menu of choices is written by someone else.",
"Courts and legislators have struggled to fit this arrangement into existing categories. Employment law was built around a simple distinction: employees, who work under direction and receive protection, and independent contractors, who run their own businesses and bear their own risks. Platform workers fall awkwardly between the two. They lack the protections of employees, yet they have little of the independence that makes self-employment a business rather than a job.",
"Some countries have responded by creating an intermediate category, granting rights such as a minimum wage for time actually worked, or access to accident insurance, without requiring fixed hours. The approach has critics. Platforms argue that it raises costs and will reduce the number of jobs; some unions argue that it entrenches a second-class status. But the alternative — forcing a new kind of work into one of two old boxes — has proved unworkable. What the gig economy has exposed is not only a gap in protection but the age of the categories through which protection is offered."],
[("The author uses the phrase ‘the menu of choices is written by someone else’ to suggest that",
  ["workers' choices are real but limited by rules the platform controls", "workers have no freedom at all", "platforms let workers set their own prices", "workers prefer fixed hours"],
  "workers' choices are real but limited by rules the platform controls",
  "The paragraph shows choices shaped by hidden algorithms, pay changes and ratings; ‘The worker is free to choose’ rules out ‘no freedom at all’.\nPassage signal: medium — economics and law, clear structure, one question on a metaphor."),
 ("According to the passage, platform workers ‘fall awkwardly between’ two categories because they",
  ["lack employees' protections and also lack the independence of a real business", "work for several platforms at once", "earn more than employees but less than contractors", "are directed by managers but bear their own risks"],
  "lack employees' protections and also lack the independence of a real business",
  "Paragraph 3, last sentence."),
 ("Which of the following is an objection to the intermediate category mentioned in the passage?",
  ["It may lock platform workers into a lower status", "It requires workers to keep fixed hours", "It removes the minimum wage", "It gives platforms too little control over ratings"],
  "It may lock platform workers into a lower status",
  "‘some unions argue that it entrenches a second-class status’. The category explicitly does not require fixed hours."),
 ("The last sentence of the passage implies that",
  ["the old legal categories of work may themselves need rethinking", "the gig economy should be banned", "platform workers are adequately protected", "unions and platforms now agree on the solution"],
  "the old legal categories of work may themselves need rethinking",
  "The gig economy has exposed ‘the age of the categories through which protection is offered.’")])

